-- Q1712: Audit Reports [easy/L1]
-- Issues: #3
UPDATE questions SET
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (B): Under AU-C 700, the management responsibility section of the auditor''s report explicitly states that management is responsible for the preparation and fair presentation of the financial statements in accordance with the applicable framework, as well as for internal control relevant to that preparation.
Wrong (A): Conducting the audit in accordance with GAAS is the auditor''s responsibility, not management''s, as described in the auditor''s responsibility section of the report.
Wrong (C): Determining materiality levels is an auditor judgment exercised during audit planning, not a management responsibility.
Wrong (D): Communicating deficiencies to those charged with governance is the auditor''s responsibility under AU-C 265.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 1712;

-- Q1713: Audit Reports [easy/L2]
-- Issues: #3
UPDATE questions SET
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (A): Under AU-C 705.08, when a qualified opinion is issued due to a material GAAP departure, the opinion paragraph uses the phrase ''except for'' the effects of the matter giving rise to the qualification. This language limits the exception to the specific departure while expressing an opinion on the remainder of the financial statements.
Wrong (B): Inability to obtain sufficient appropriate evidence describes a scope limitation, not a GAAP departure; scope limitations use different wording.
Wrong (C): The opinion paragraph is not replaced—it is modified with ''except for'' language, and a Basis for Qualified Opinion paragraph is added separately.
Wrong (D): A disclaimer of opinion applies when the auditor cannot obtain sufficient evidence and the effects are pervasive, not when a specific GAAP departure is identified.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 1713;

-- Q1714: Audit Reports [easy/L2]
-- Issues: #3
UPDATE questions SET
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (A): Under AU-C 705.08, when a scope limitation results in possible effects that are material but not pervasive to the financial statements, the auditor issues a qualified opinion. The ''except for'' language acknowledges the limitation while still expressing an opinion on the remaining statements.
Wrong (B): An unmodified opinion requires the auditor to have obtained sufficient appropriate evidence; a material scope limitation precludes this.
Wrong (C): An adverse opinion under AU-C 705.09 applies when misstatements are both material and pervasive—it addresses known misstatements, not scope limitations.
Wrong (D): A disclaimer of opinion under AU-C 705.10 applies when the scope limitation is so severe that the possible effects are both material and pervasive.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 1714;

-- Q1717: Audit Reports [easy/L1]
-- Issues: #3
UPDATE questions SET
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (A): Under AU-C 706.06, an emphasis-of-matter paragraph may be included when the auditor considers it necessary to draw users'' attention to a matter that is appropriately presented or disclosed in the financial statements and is fundamental to users'' understanding. The matter must already be adequately disclosed.
Wrong (B): An emphasis-of-matter paragraph does not modify the auditor''s opinion—it draws attention to a properly disclosed matter without affecting the opinion type.
Wrong (C): The auditor does not need written permission from management to include an emphasis-of-matter paragraph; this is solely the auditor''s judgment.
Wrong (D): Emphasis-of-matter paragraphs relate to disclosed matters, not scope limitations; scope limitations are addressed through opinion modifications under AU-C 705.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 1717;

-- Q1718: Audit Reports [medium/L2]
-- Issues: #3
UPDATE questions SET
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (D): Under AU-C 706.06, a change in accounting principle that has a material effect on the financial statements requires an emphasis-of-matter paragraph to draw attention to the note describing the change. Switching from FIFO to LIFO is a voluntary accounting change requiring this disclosure.
Wrong (A): A fully disclosed related-party transaction may warrant an emphasis-of-matter paragraph but is not as likely to require one as an accounting principle change, which AU-C 708 specifically addresses.
Wrong (B): Management''s refusal to sign the representation letter is a scope limitation under AU-C 580 that may lead to a disclaimer, not an emphasis-of-matter paragraph.
Wrong (C): Inability to observe inventory is a scope limitation addressed under AU-C 705 through opinion modification, not through an emphasis-of-matter paragraph.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 1718;

-- Q1723: Audit Reports [easy/L1]
-- Issues: #3
UPDATE questions SET
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (D): Under AU-C 560.14, before reissuing a report on prior-period financial statements, the predecessor auditor should read the current-period financial statements, read the successor auditor''s report, obtain a representation letter from current management, and compare the prior-period statements with those previously reported on. These procedures help identify events that may affect the previously issued report.
Wrong (A): Accepting without additional procedures could result in reissuing a report that is no longer appropriate due to subsequent events or restatements.
Wrong (B): A complete re-audit of the prior period is not required—the specified procedures under AU-C 560 are sufficient.
Wrong (C): A new engagement letter from the audit committee is not among the required procedures; the predecessor auditor''s procedures focus on reading current information.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 1723;

-- Q1728: Audit Reports [easy/L1]
-- Issues: #3
UPDATE questions SET
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (C): Under AU-C 570.21, the going concern emphasis-of-matter paragraph must reference the note in the financial statements that describes the conditions giving rise to substantial doubt, management''s evaluation of those conditions, and management''s plans to address them.
Wrong (A): The emphasis paragraph references the disclosure note, not specific projected dollar amounts—detailed loss projections belong in the financial statement disclosures themselves.
Wrong (B): Auditors do not predict a specific cessation date; the paragraph addresses doubt about continuity, not a timeline for closure.
Wrong (D): Management''s personal guarantee of survival is not a recognized element of going concern disclosure under AU-C 570.$EXPL$
