-- Q1730: Audit Reports [easy/L2]
-- Issues: #3
UPDATE questions SET
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (B): Under AU-C 800, when financial statements are prepared using a special purpose framework such as the tax basis, the auditor can issue an unmodified opinion if the statements are fairly presented within that framework. The report includes an emphasis-of-matter paragraph identifying the framework and referring to the relevant note.
Wrong (A): A qualified opinion for departure from GAAP is inappropriate because AU-C 800 recognizes tax basis as a legitimate special purpose framework—it is not a GAAP departure.
Wrong (C): An adverse opinion is unwarranted because the financial statements are being evaluated against the tax-basis framework, not GAAP.
Wrong (D): A disclaimer is inappropriate because the auditor can obtain sufficient evidence to opine on statements prepared under a special purpose framework.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 1730;

-- Q1732: Audit Reports [medium/L2]
-- Issues: #3
UPDATE questions SET
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (A): Under AU-C 705, the distinction between a qualified opinion and a disclaimer when a scope limitation exists depends on the pervasiveness of the possible effects. Material but not pervasive effects warrant a qualified opinion, while material and pervasive effects—affecting multiple elements or a substantial proportion of the statements—warrant a disclaimer.
Wrong (B): Whether the client or external circumstances caused the limitation does not determine the opinion type; the auditor focuses on the potential magnitude of undetected misstatements.
Wrong (C): The distinction is not based on whether the limitation relates to current-year or prior-year balances but on how broadly the missing evidence could affect the financial statements.
Wrong (D): Issuance of a management letter is unrelated to the choice between a qualified opinion and a disclaimer; the decision rests on pervasiveness of possible effects.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 1732;

-- Q1734: Audit Reports [easy/L1]
-- Issues: #3
UPDATE questions SET
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (C): Under AU-C 705.08, the ''except for'' language is the hallmark of a qualified opinion. It indicates that the auditor identified a matter that is material but not pervasive, carving out that specific exception while expressing an opinion on the remainder of the financial statements.
Wrong (A): A disclaimer of opinion states that the auditor does not express an opinion at all, typically due to pervasive scope limitations—it does not use ''except for'' language.
Wrong (B): An adverse opinion states that the financial statements are not fairly presented due to material and pervasive misstatements—it uses ''do not present fairly'' language, not ''except for.''
Wrong (D): An unmodified opinion with emphasis-of-matter contains no ''except for'' qualification; the emphasis paragraph draws attention without modifying the opinion.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 1734;

-- Q1739: Audit Reports [easy/L1]
-- Issues: #3
UPDATE questions SET
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (B): Under AU-C 710, when a prior-year scope limitation has been resolved through procedures performed in the current year, the auditor updates the opinion on the prior-period financial statements based on the results of those additional procedures. The auditor is not required to maintain the original opinion if circumstances have changed.
Wrong (A): Maintaining the original opinion regardless of new evidence is inappropriate—AU-C 710 requires the auditor to update the opinion when the limitation is resolved.
Wrong (C): Automatically issuing a qualified opinion regardless of results ignores the purpose of resolving the limitation; the updated opinion depends on what the additional procedures reveal.
Wrong (D): An adverse opinion addresses material and pervasive misstatements, not resolved scope limitations—it would be inappropriate here.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 1739;

-- Q1740: Audit Reports [easy/L1]
-- Issues: #3
UPDATE questions SET
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (C): Under AU-C 560, when a subsequent event occurs between the report date and the date the report is released, the auditor may either dual-date the report (keeping the original date except for the specific event) or extend the report date to the later date. Both approaches fulfill the auditor''s responsibility for subsequent events.
Wrong (A): The auditor has an active responsibility for subsequent events discovered before the report is released and cannot simply ignore them under AU-C 560.
Wrong (B): A completely new audit report with a new engagement letter is not required—the original engagement covers the reporting period, and dual-dating or extending the date suffices.
Wrong (D): Adding a disclaimer paragraph is not the appropriate response; the auditor addresses subsequent events through dual-dating or date extension, not through disclaimers.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 1740;

-- Q1741: Audit Reports [easy/L1]
-- Issues: #3
UPDATE questions SET
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (A): Under AU-C 706 and ASC 450, a contingency assessed as reasonably possible and properly disclosed in the notes does not require modification of the auditor''s opinion. GAAP requires note disclosure for reasonably possible contingencies, and if management has complied, the auditor issues an unmodified opinion.
Wrong (B): A qualified opinion is not warranted because proper disclosure of a reasonably possible contingency satisfies GAAP requirements—there is no misstatement or scope limitation.
Wrong (C): A disclaimer is reserved for situations where the auditor cannot obtain sufficient evidence, not for properly disclosed contingencies with adequate information available.
Wrong (D): An adverse opinion addresses material and pervasive misstatements in the financial statements; a properly disclosed contingency is not a misstatement.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 1741;

-- Q1747: Audit Reports [easy/L1]
-- Issues: #3
UPDATE questions SET
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (C): Under AU-C 708, when prior-year financial statements are restated to correct a material misstatement, the auditor adds an emphasis-of-matter paragraph referencing the note that describes the restatement. This alerts users to the correction and its effects on the comparative financial statements.
Wrong (A): Ignoring the restatement is inappropriate because the current report on comparative statements must address the restated prior-year figures under AU-C 708.
Wrong (B): An adverse opinion on the current year is unwarranted—the restatement corrects the prior year and does not indicate a misstatement in the current-year financial statements.
