-- Q1752: Review and Compilation [easy/L1]
-- Issues: #3
UPDATE questions SET
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (B): Under AR-C 90, a review engagement provides limited (negative) assurance. The accountant states that nothing came to their attention causing them to believe the financial statements are materially misstated. This is less than an audit but more than a compilation.
Wrong (A): Reasonable assurance is provided by audit engagements under AU-C standards, which require extensive substantive testing and evidence gathering beyond inquiry and analytical procedures.
Wrong (C): Absolute assurance is not provided by any type of engagement—even audits provide only reasonable, not absolute, assurance due to inherent limitations.
Wrong (D): No assurance describes a compilation engagement under AR-C 80, where the accountant assists in presenting financial statements without providing any assurance.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 1752;

-- Q1753: Review and Compilation [easy/L1]
-- Issues: #3
UPDATE questions SET
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (A): Under AR-C 90.24, the two principal procedures in a review engagement are inquiry of management and analytical procedures. These form the basis for obtaining limited assurance that no material modifications are needed to the financial statements.
Wrong (B): Inspection of documents and observation of processes are audit procedures under AU-C 500, not review procedures—they exceed the scope of a review engagement.
Wrong (C): Confirmation of account balances and vouching of transactions are substantive audit procedures under AU-C 330 that go beyond the limited scope of a review.
Wrong (D): Physical examination of assets and recalculation of computations are also audit-level procedures that exceed the scope of review engagements under AR-C 90.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 1753;

-- Q1756: Review and Compilation [medium/L1]
-- Issues: #3
UPDATE questions SET
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (B): Under AR-C 80.20 and AR-C 70.05, the key difference is that a compilation results in the issuance of a compilation report, while a preparation engagement does not require a report—only a legend on each page of the financial statements. Neither engagement provides assurance.
Wrong (A): Both compilation and preparation engagements require engagement letters under AR-C 80.10 and AR-C 70.06, so this is not a distinguishing factor.
Wrong (C): Neither a compilation nor a preparation provides assurance on the financial statements—compilations provide no assurance, and preparations similarly provide none.
Wrong (D): Independence is not required for either a compilation or a preparation engagement under AR-C 80.24 and AR-C 70, though lack of independence must be disclosed in a compilation report.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 1756;

-- Q1762: Review and Compilation [medium/L2]
-- Issues: #3
UPDATE questions SET
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (A): Under AR-C 90.38–.39, when the accountant becomes aware of a material departure from the applicable framework and management refuses to revise, the accountant should modify the review report to include a separate paragraph describing the departure and, if practicable, its effects on the financial statements.
Wrong (B): Issuing an unmodified report when a known material departure exists would fail to communicate the issue to users and violates AR-C 90.38.
Wrong (C): Withdrawal is required only when the departure is designed to mislead users, not merely when management disagrees with the accountant about a framework departure.
Wrong (D): Converting to a compilation does not resolve the issue—the material departure still exists, and the accountant''s awareness of it must be communicated regardless of service level.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 1762;

